# PyInstaller build artifacts
build/
dist/
*.spec
!MEBP.spec

# Local virtual environment
.venv/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.egg-info/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Runtime / user data
settings.json
settings.json.bak-*
print_resume.json
current_button_mapping.json
print_records/
config/sim_xy_state.json
config/sim_xy_state_ludl.json
config/sim_zp_state.json
xy_debug_*.csv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-machine state — NEVER share between rigs.
#
# v7.17.x: this used to be a hand-maintained list of exact filenames, which
# fell behind (config/hardware/well_training/ and several other per-machine
# calibration stores were being tracked in git — the actual cause of the
# device-specific pull conflicts this restructuring fixes). It is now
# structural instead: every per-machine store lives under its own
# config/hardware/<machine-id>/ folder (see SupportClasses/MachineConfig.py +
# config/machine_id.txt, which names the folder), and EVERYTHING under
# config/hardware/ is per-machine EXCEPT config/hardware/ME3B_general/, the
# shared/portable catalogues (needles, syringes, cameras, plate/needle/well/
# target types, plates, inserts, swappable *Setup*.json hardware configs)
# meant to travel between machines. A brand-new per-machine store added later
# is covered automatically as long as it resolves its path through
# resolve_machine_path()/resolve_shared_path() — no further edits needed here.
#
# Carrying one rig's per-machine values onto another is the failure this
# codebase has been bitten by repeatedly (see the CAMERA_CAL_PERSIST_STORE and
# PLATE_ORIENTATION notes in CLAUDE.md) — a stale calibration silently drives
# the needle to the wrong place.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
config/hardware/*/
!config/hardware/ME3B_general/
!config/hardware/ME3B_general/**
# Device profiles NAME the per-machine folders, so they cannot live inside one.
# Tracked deliberately: each rig's profile has a distinct filename so they can
# never conflict on pull, and it backs up every rig's safety envelope /
# steps-per-mm / axis map for a rebuild. Loading one is always an explicit
# operator action on Hardware Setup, so no rig adopts another's settings.
!config/hardware/devices/
!config/hardware/devices/**
config/hardware/**/*.bak-*

# Per-machine UI/session state outside config/hardware/.
config/context_panel_layout.json
config/workflows/*/__last__.json

# Application logs, including the rotated copies. Tracking these put ~500k
# lines of churn into a single commit.
logs/*.log
logs/*.log.[0-9]
logs/*.log.[0-9][0-9]

# Print execution logs (keep the schema README, ignore the runs)
logs/prints/*.jsonl

# Deliberately KEPT: logs/hwtest, logs/challenge and logs/timing hold
# hardware-run records that the update plans cite as evidence.

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/

# Testing
.pytest_cache/

# Walkthrough recordings (REC button): one full-window PNG per click,
# so a single take is tens of MB of binaries.
logs/sessions/

# HyperFrames tutorial videos — commit the composition + generated CSS,
# ignore the toolchain, rendered MP4s and review snapshots.
docs/videos/*/node_modules/
docs/videos/*/out/
docs/videos/*/snapshots/
docs/videos/*/.hyperframes/

# v7.14 operator captures (📷 / ⏺ buttons on every live feed): full-resolution
# 16-bit TIFFs and video are large experiment data, not source. This is only
# the DEFAULT destination — the capture settings dialog can point anywhere.
captures/

# v7.16 .nd3 exports written by the end-to-end checks in logs/nd3/. The rest of
# logs/ is covered by `logs/*.log` above, which does not match these — a 16 MB
# HDF5 container of one plate's imagery is experiment output, not source.
logs/nd3/

# Scratch directory a test run moved stray plate documents into. Debris, not a
# fixture: the plate-document tests build what they need in a tmpdir.
.trash-testdebris/
